# Wick

## Overview

Wick is a survival game played in the browser. A lamplighter is alone in a
moth-choked city at night, and everything in the dark is drawn to the light.
The player only moves. The lamplighter's tools fire on their own, each on its
own rhythm, and what the player decides is where to stand and what to take at
each level-up. The night lasts ten minutes: reach dawn and the run is won; run
out of health and the lamplighter has fallen.

Every common enemy killed drops a gem, gems fill an experience bar, and each level
offers a choice of three: a new tool, a new trinket, or a level on one already
held, across `WEAPON_SLOTS` (`6`) tool slots and `PASSIVE_SLOTS` (`6`) trinket
slots. A tool at its top level, carried beside the trinket it pairs with,
transforms into a stronger form when a chest is opened. Chests come from the
elites that arrive on a schedule, and the night's last arrival is the Dark, a
pursuer the lamplighter outruns until dawn.

Wick has its own name, look, and rules, and its assets, branding, and design
are original to it.

The specification spans several files under `specs/`. `specs/world.md` gives
the world frame, the camera, the order of a tick and how timers count, the
lamplighter's movement, facing, contact damage, recovery, the run's two
endings, and the gems and pickups the field carries; `specs/weapons.md` the
ten weapons, their targeting, and their level tables; `specs/evolutions.md`
the six evolved weapons, their recipes, and what a chest's result is;
`specs/passives.md` the ten passives and the derived stats they feed;
`specs/progression.md` experience, levels, slots, the level-up offers, and the
two overlays; `specs/enemies.md` the enemy roster, their behaviors, and the
spawn director; `specs/controls.md` the key actions and what each screen reads
of them; `specs/state.md` what the game's state carries;
`specs/instrumentation.md` the debug and automation surface; `specs/ui.md` the
screens, the HUD, and the audio cues; and `specs/assets.md` the sprites and
sounds the build produces and ships. Read all of them and build the game they
describe as one cohesive whole.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The runtime

This game stands on no engine, so the runtime under it is yours to write: the
frame loop and the delta time it measures, the canvas fit, the keyboard, audio
cues including the ones that loop, loading the produced sprites and sounds, and
the debug overlay. Where this specification speaks of the runtime it means that
layer, and it states only what Wick needs from it. How the layer is structured
is yours to decide.
{{else}}
## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It owns the frame loop and
the delta time the game is given, the input actions the lamplighter and the
menus are driven by, audio cues including the ones that loop, asset loading,
the canvas fit, and the debug overlay. Read its documentation before you start.
This specification states only what is specific to Wick.

The game is played from the keyboard alone. The engine hands `update` each
action as a held value and as a press edge; `specs/controls.md` states what
Wick does with them.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested
with Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain is
fixed and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`. The directory does not exist yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is where
your build starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop and the
canvas fit, keyboard input, audio, asset loading, the diagnostics overlay, the
debug and automation surface, the figures this specification fixes, and the
game itself.

The game's state is yours to shape. `specs/state.md` states what it must carry,
and `specs/instrumentation.md` states the one contract fixed over it.

### What stays as it is

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the stage is fitted into.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need, added
along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and scripts stay
as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, identifier, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is stated here under a name. Name each one once in a module
of your own and read it from there, rather than restating a value at each use.
Anything this specification does not name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured
over `src/`. A test drives the game over its own clock for a counted number of
ticks, so it needs no browser. Cover the parts of the game a person cannot see
at a glance: the movement vector and facing, each weapon's targeting and
cooldown, the derived stats, the experience curve and the offer pool, the
evolution recipes and the chest fallback, the spawn director's windows and
scripted events, and contact damage with armor and the two endings.
{{else}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<WickState, WickDebugApi>`: `initialize`
builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them together as
`[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a read-only view
(`DeepReadonly<WickState>`) and returns the next state, advanced against the
frame's delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed that next state,
read-only again, and draws it. The engine holds the state by value and replaces
it with what each `update` returns, so a frame builds the next state from the
current one rather than writing into it, and nothing but `update` and the debug
surface's poses advances the game. All three functions throw as they stand.
Implement them, and split the work across new files under `src/` however suits
you.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `WickState` and the types it is built
from, exactly as `specs/state.md` states them, and `WickDebugApi`, the debug
and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the canvas is
cleared to each frame, so the letterbox bars around the stage match the night.
The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color your night
uses.

